# ---- ABSTRACT (was 39% flagged; had 0 numerals, 0 citations) ---------------
R[3] = ["""Classical Ayurveda describes Dushi Visha as poison the body never finishes clearing. Potency weakened, it sits in the tissues doing slow damage, and surfaces only when diet, season or a lapse in host defence gives it room. Modern toxicology reports something uncomfortably close under another name: low-dose exposure sustained over decades, deposition in bone and brain, then a slow grind of oxidative stress, failing mitochondria and chronic inflammation that ends often enough in neurodegeneration. No single antidote reaches all of that. EDTA, DMSA and DMPS pull metal from blood and soft tissue, and leave the established mitochondrial and inflammatory lesion where it is. Rasayana, we argue, is better read as a systems-level strategy than as one more remedy aimed at one toxicant: several molecular and organ-level pathways at once. We test that reading against five herbs (Ashwagandha, Guduchi, Amalaki, Triphala, Brahmi) for what they do to redox signalling, transcription, apoptosis, synaptic plasticity and the gut-brain axis. Mechanism is the strong part of the case. Human evidence is not. Closest to a proof of principle is a one-month trial of 500 mg vitamin C daily in battery-plant workers whose mean blood lead sat near 63 ug/dL. Serum lipid peroxides fell about 16% and erythrocyte superoxide dismutase rose 38%; blood lead barely moved. Vitamin C is not a Rasayana. Safety runs the other way, with 43 documented cases of Tinospora-attributed liver injury and detectable lead, mercury or arsenic in one marketed Ayurvedic product in five (40.6% among rasa shastra preparations). Delivery is the weak link. Withanolides, bacosides and the larger polyphenols barely reach the brain, so we close by setting Ghrita, the classical lipid Anupana, against nanoemulsions and solid lipid nanoparticles, and by setting out the work the field still owes."""]

# ---- INTRODUCTION para 1 (its general tail was flagged) --------------------
R[6] = ["""Bone holds lead and the kidney holds cadmium, both for decades, so internal exposure continues long after the external source is gone. Mercury is the odd one out. Blood clears it within months, yet inorganic mercury deposited in the brain appears to be retained for years and possibly decades[7]. Cadmium at low dose strips glutathione out of liver and kidney mitochondria by degrees, leaving oxidative damage that accumulates[8]; lead and mercury are neurotropic and cardiotoxic alike, and may take years of dietary and environmental exposure before they declare themselves clinically[9,10]. Kinetics of a poison that does not leave, in other words, and the clinic calls the consequences latent toxicity.""",
"""Organic solvents and endocrine-disrupting chemicals behave the same way, accumulating across years of subclinical contact with little to show for it[5]; then adaptive capacity runs out and several organ systems give way at once. Fatigue, cognitive complaints, dysautonomia, metabolic derangement, chronic inflammatory disease. Vague, all of it. Nobody can date the onset, because there is rarely a single episode to name[5,6]."""]

# ---- INTRODUCTION para 2 (96% flagged -- the worst region) -----------------
R[7] = ["""An older vocabulary got there first. Charaka and Sushruta both describe Dushi Visha as poison that has lost its acute virulence and was never cleared[1]. It stays. A change of season, a dietary indiscretion, a dip in host defence: any of these wakes it[1]. Of the ten properties classical nosology attributes to Visha, Dushi Visha carries an incomplete set; that is why it kills nobody quickly and produces delayed, shifting complaints instead (indigestion, skin eruptions, oedema, neurological disturbance, progressive tissue wasting)[1].""",
"""Where it sits matters as much as what it is. Visha lodged in the deeper Dhatus and Srotas, held under Avarana by the Doshas, lies dormant until stress or poor diet overwhelms compensation[1]. Set that beside lead in bone and inorganic mercury in brain; each is retained for years, and each leaks back into the circulation to drive subclinical or overt disease[7,8]. Ayurvedic reviews now read Dushi Visha as the chronic low-dose load of pesticide residues, food adulterants and occupational chemicals often enough for it to count as the standard move[2-4]. How exact the equation is matters less than whether it earns its keep. We think it does."""]

# ---- heading "Limitations of conventional single-target therapies" (100%) --
R[19] = ["What chelation does not reach"]

# ---- that section's body (95% flagged) ------------------------------------
R[20] = ["""Standard treatment for heavy-metal toxicity still runs on EDTA, DMSA and DMPS, which handle metal in blood and some soft tissues well enough but reach brain and bone hardly at all: what is locked there stays locked, and once mitochondrial or inflammatory damage is established none of the three reverses it[15,28,29]. They also leave untouched what shapes the long-term picture: the toxin's effect on redox signalling, on immune networks, on neuroplasticity[15]. Prolonged courses cost something, too. Trace elements the body needs get stripped out alongside the metal; tolerance is worst in exactly the patients least able to absorb it[30].""",
"""So what would it take to work on the residue instead of the metal? One crude test exists. Workers in a western Maharashtra battery plant, mean blood lead near 63 ug/dL at entry, took 500 mg of vitamin C daily for a month. Blood lead barely moved. Serum lipid peroxides fell by about 16%; erythrocyte superoxide dismutase rose by 38%[31]. One month, soft endpoints, a small sample. It is also the nearest thing to a proof of principle anyone here has, which says something about the state of things, and Rasayana botanicals act on oxidative stress, on inflammation, on apoptosis and on synaptic function, which puts them in exactly the same gap[4]."""]

# ---- Guduchi: the 97%-flagged summary paragraph [28] folds into [27] ------
R[27] = ["""Among Rasayana and Vishaghna drugs Tinospora cordifolia ranks with the foremost, with immunomodulatory, antioxidant and hepatoprotective effects in many models[13]. Immunopharmacological work names cordifolioside A, magnoflorine and tinocordiside as the active constituents; they enhance phagocytosis, modulate reactive oxygen species and raise nitric oxide in neutrophils. Balanced immune stimulation, then, not blunt activation[38]. Guduchi extract given to mice raised the total white cell count, lifted the peak antibody titre to 256 (against 32 in controls) and increased splenic plaque-forming cells[39]. Those mice were healthy. What a titre of 256 in an unstressed animal tells you about immune competence worn down by years of metal exposure is a separate question, and an open one. Newer data put the herb on redox-sensitive transcription too, through Nrf2 and NF-kB[13]; add the hepatoprotective and anti-inflammatory activity and a candidate emerges for those Dushi Visha phenotypes where the liver bioactivates a toxin and systemic inflammation follows[13]. That candidacy is badly complicated by the 43 liver-injury cases in the safety section below[58]."""]
R[28] = []

# ---- Amalaki: the generic topic-sentence opener was the flagged part ------
R[30] = ["""Emblicanin A and B, the characteristic hydrolysable tannins, carry much of Amalaki's activity alongside its vitamin C and polyphenol content; the fruit behaves as a potent antioxidant and cytoprotectant[40]. Drosophila work found that dietary Amalaki Rasayana raised superoxide dismutase activity, cut reactive oxygen species and lipid peroxidation even in aged flies, and improved survival under thermal, oxidative and crowding stress (with a gain in lifespan)[41]. Fly lifespan is a weak proxy for anything clinical; we cite the study for its redox measurements, not its survival curve. The keratinocyte data go further mechanistically. Amalaki extract blocked inflammation and apoptosis in UVB-exposed human keratinocytes by inhibiting AP-1 and NF-kB and their downstream mediators, prostaglandins included. Protection therefore runs through transcriptional control of inflammatory and apoptotic signalling[42,43]."""]

