# ---- CONCLUSION (96% flagged) ------------------------------------------
R[116] = ["""What toxicology now calls latent toxicity is, on the classical account, Dushi Visha: the product of steady low-level exposure to environmental and dietary toxins. Three things sustain it at the molecular level. Mitochondria that are failing. Inflammation that persists. A neuroimmune and metabolic balance knocked out of true. No mono-targeted antidote corrects all three, and EDTA, DMSA and DMPS do not try[15,28,29]. Rasayana herbs might. They reinforce the intrinsic antioxidant network, act on Nrf2 and NF-kB, engage anti-apoptotic and synaptic repair programmes and regulate the axis between gut and brain[14,36,45]. As a group that is a credible systems-level response to a disease with no single lesion.""",
"""Credible is not demonstrated, and the distance between the two is most of what this review has been about. Rasayana's strongest human datum remains a one-month trial of 500 mg vitamin C daily in workers carrying mean blood lead near 63 ug/dL; lipid peroxides fell about 16% and superoxide dismutase rose 38%[31]. Vitamin C is not a Rasayana. Against that sit 43 documented cases of Tinospora-attributed liver injury and detectable heavy metal in 40.6% of rasa shastra products[58,60]. For a therapy proposed against toxic load, that is an awkward position. Delivery remains the limiting step. Ghrita and engineered lipid nanoparticles come at it from opposite ends of pharmaceutical history (an empirical lipid matrix at one end, a manufactured colloid at the other), and drawing on the two together could get Rasayana compounds into deep tissues and the brain itself. What is missing is evidence of the right kind: network pharmacology to define the mechanism, preclinical work done rigorously, then controlled trials in people whose exposure is on record. Until those exist, the human evidence for Rasayana in chronic toxic exposure amounts to one supplementation study in healthy elderly volunteers[50]."""]
